From f825de216e073d6dfb64d68ede34ecfb051e1ed2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cinemaONE <35371193+cinema-ONE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 13:16:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [Subtitles] Let bitmap subtitles be resized and repositioned

Kodi exposes no control at all over picture based subtitles. PGS and VobSub are
drawn exactly as authored, at the size and in the place the disc chose, while
every text subtitle honours a font size, a position and a vertical margin. Two
settings close that gap, in a new "Image based subtitles" group of the subtitle
settings - group 1 there is labelled "Styles for text based subtitles" and is
the wrong home for either of them.

subtitles.bitmapzoom resizes them, 10% to 200%, default 100. Reusing the font
size setting was considered and rejected: it is a pixel height, it means
nothing for a bitmap, and someone who enlarged their text subtitles has not
asked for larger PGS. Note this is a preference, not a correction: measured
across 47 titles, a single line of PGS is 4.54% of the picture height at the
median on both Blu-ray and Ultra HD Blu-ray, because the 1920x1080 PGS canvas
of a UHD disc is stretched to the 3840x2160 picture and so keeps the same size
on screen. What that stretch costs is sharpness, not size.

subtitles.bitmapposition, default off, applies "Position on screen" and
"Vertical margin" to them as well, including "Bottom of video" and "Top of
video": those two measure from the video rectangle rather than from the screen,
so a 2.40:1 video letterboxed into a 16:9 screen keeps its subtitles inside the
picture instead of dropping them into the bar below it. Text subtitles already
work that way - for MarginsMode::INSIDE_VIDEO, CDVDSubtitlesLibass insets
libass' margins by the bar - and this is the same rule applied to bitmaps. It
is off by default because turning it on moves subtitles that are today drawn
where the disc put them, which is a change no one asked for until they ask for
it.

Placement works on the content rather than the rectangle. Each overlay records
where its visible pixels sit inside its quad; a PGS object is cropped tightly
by its encoder so that is almost always the whole quad, but a stream is free to
put a short line of text inside a large rectangle and that must not be read as
a full screen graphic. Overlays close enough together to be one block of text
move as a group, so a line of dialogue at the bottom and a translated sign near
the top are not dragged into each other. A group is moved so that the edge
nearest the screen edge it is read against lands on the target line - the
bottom edge for the lower half of the picture, the top edge for the upper half
- because that is the edge a reader's eye returns to, and anchoring the centre
instead would make the baseline jump between a one line and a two line cue.
A top position sends every block to the top. A cue the disc put at the top
stays there under a bottom position too, as the disc placed it clear of
something at the bottom of the picture. Blocks bound for the same edge are
stacked in the order the disc placed them, so dialogue and a sign shown
together never overlap.
Anything taller than a third of the picture, or straddling the middle of it, is
a graphic and is left alone, and an offset is never allowed to push a subtitle
off the screen.

Resizing anchors the same way, so changing the size does not also move the text
off its line.

Both settings only ever apply to overlays marked as bitmap subtitles, so
Blu-ray menus, DVD button highlights and DVB station graphics are untouched.

The zoom setting is a slider with format="integer" and a "{0:d} %" format
label, not format="percentage": CGUIControlSliderSetting turns the latter into
a percentage slider that is hard clamped to 0-100 and ignores the setting's own
range, so nothing above 100% could be selected. The name and range match the
setting CoreELEC's p3i builds have been shipping downstream, so that a rebase
there can drop their patch rather than end up carrying two settings that do the
same thing.

Verified end to end against a PGS track from a real remux, under Xvfb, against
an unpatched control: the patched build with default settings renders a byte
identical frame to unpatched master; at 60% the subtitle is visibly smaller and
at 200% visibly larger, both with the baseline and the horizontal centre held;
with the position setting on and a 15% margin it lifts by the expected 110px;
on the same clip with its PGS moved to the top of the frame it moves down to
the margin instead; and on a 1920x804 encode letterboxed into a 1920x1080
window, where the picture spans rows 138 to 941, "Bottom of video" puts the
subtitle's last row at 883 - the picture bottom less the 53px margin - where
"Bottom of screen" puts it at 1021 and the setting turned off leaves it
straddling the picture edge at 1014.

